---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";



const moreInCategory = [
    { title: "How Long Does Driveway Sealer Last?", url: "/how-long-does-driveway-sealer-last/" },
    { title: "How Much Does It Cost to Seal a Driveway? (2026 Price Guide)", url: "/how-much-does-it-cost-to-seal-a-driveway/" },
    { title: "How to Clean an Epoxy Garage Floor", url: "/how-to-clean-epoxy-garage-floor/" },
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-05-19", modified: "2026-05-19" });
const title = "Garage Floor Coating Cost: DIY vs Professional Installation";
const description =
  "What garage floor coatings actually cost in 2026 \u2014 paint, epoxy, polyaspartic, and polyurea compared, DIY vs.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Cost Guides",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Garage Floor Coating Cost",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/garage-floor-coating-cost/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What's the cheapest garage floor coating that actually holds up?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A two-part water-based epoxy kit is the cheapest option that delivers real, lasting protection, running $120-180 for a one-car garage. One-part floor paint is cheaper upfront at $50-90 for a one-car garage, but it isn't a true coating system — it has no chemical bond to concrete, wears through in high-traffic areas within 1-2 years, and needs recoating on a near-annual basis in a daily-driver garage. If you want the lowest true cost of ownership rather than just the lowest sticker price, a DIY epoxy kit beats one-part paint even though it costs more on day one.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much more does polyaspartic cost than epoxy?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Professional polyaspartic systems run $8-12 per square foot installed versus $3-8 per square foot for professional epoxy, so a two-car garage typically costs $1,500-2,000 more for polyaspartic than for a comparable epoxy system. The premium buys faster cure time (often ready for vehicle traffic in 24 hours versus 3-7 days for epoxy), better UV stability so it won't yellow, and a longer expected lifespan of 15-25+ years versus 5-15 years for epoxy. On a cost-per-year basis the gap narrows considerably because polyaspartic lasts so much longer.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is polyurea worth the extra cost over polyaspartic and epoxy?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Polyurea is typically the most expensive professional option at $9-14 per square foot installed, slightly above polyaspartic. It's worth the extra cost primarily for garages that see extreme temperature swings, heavy moisture exposure, or high foot and vehicle traffic, since polyurea stays flexible across a wider temperature range and resists moisture-related failures better than epoxy or polyaspartic. For a typical residential garage in a moderate climate, the performance difference between top-tier polyaspartic and polyurea is small enough that price and contractor availability usually decide the choice rather than raw performance.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does garage size change the cost per square foot?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Per-square-foot pricing generally drops as garage size increases because mobilization, equipment setup, and minimum-job charges get spread across more square footage. A contractor might charge $6 per square foot for a small one-car garage but $4.50-5 per square foot for the same coating system on a three-car garage. Most professional installers also have a minimum project charge — often $600-900 — which makes very small garages proportionally more expensive per square foot than the mid-size jobs they're set up to run efficiently.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What drives garage floor coating costs up the most, regardless of material?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Surface preparation is the single biggest cost driver across every coating type. Diamond grinding a heavily stained or previously coated floor can double prep time and add $200-750 to a quote no matter which coating goes on top. After prep, the next biggest factors are crack repair (an extra $30-400 depending on severity), moisture vapor barrier treatment if the floor tests positive ($300-800 professionally), and regional labor rates, which swing identical jobs by 40-60% between low-cost and high-cost markets. The coating material itself is often a smaller share of total cost than these preparation-related factors.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "BlogPosting",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
      description: description,
      mainEntityOfPage: {
        "@type": "WebPage",
        "@id": "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/garage-floor-coating-cost/",
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "garage floor coating cost",
      "garage floor epoxy",
      "polyaspartic coating",
      "polyurea coating",
      "garage floor paint",
      "home improvement cost guide",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "Compare Coating Costs",
    link: "#cost-at-a-glance",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Cost Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated May 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Garage Floor Coating Cost: <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">DIY vs Professional Installation</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Paint, epoxy, polyaspartic, and polyurea compared side by side — real per-square-foot pricing, DIY vs. professional cost gaps, and what actually drives the price up no matter which coating you choose.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#cost-at-a-glance"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            Compare Coating Costs
          </a>
          <a
            href="#cost-per-year"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Cost Per Year of Protection
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="16.8k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#cost-at-a-glance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Cost at a Glance (All Coating Types)</a>
          <a href="#per-sq-ft" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Per-Square-Foot Pricing Compared</a>
          <a href="#diy-vs-pro-gap" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The DIY vs. Professional Cost Gap</a>
          <a href="#cost-drivers" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. What Drives Cost Up, Regardless of Coating</a>
          <a href="#decision-framework" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. DIY vs. Pro Decision Framework</a>
          <a href="#cost-per-year" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Cost Per Year of Protection</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- Pull Quote -->
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          The biggest cost variable in a garage floor coating project isn't which material you pick — it's whether you do it yourself or hire it out. That single decision swings a two-car garage from around $250 to over $7,000 for a comparable finish.
        </p>

        <p>
          "Garage floor coating" isn't one product — it's a category that spans four fundamentally different systems with very different price tags and lifespans: one-part floor paint, two-part epoxy, polyaspartic, and polyurea. Most cost guides online only cover epoxy, which leaves out half the picture. This guide puts all four side by side so you can see where your money actually goes and where the long-term value is, whichever coating you end up choosing.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1: Cost at a Glance -->
        <div id="cost-at-a-glance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Garage Floor Coating Cost at a Glance
          </h2>
          <p>
            The table below shows realistic total-project cost across every major coating type and the most common garage sizes. DIY figures are materials only; professional figures include materials and labor. None of these numbers include crack repair or moisture remediation — those are covered in the cost-drivers section below, since they apply regardless of which coating you pick.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
            <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Coating Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">One-Car (~250 sq ft)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Two-Car (~500 sq ft)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Three-Car (~750 sq ft)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">One-Part Paint (DIY)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$90–$150</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$175–$300</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$260–$450</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">DIY Epoxy Kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$130–$400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$250–$800</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$375–$1,200</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Professional Epoxy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$750–$2,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,500–$4,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2,250–$6,000</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Professional Polyaspartic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2,000–$3,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$4,000–$6,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$6,000–$9,000</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Professional Polyurea</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2,250–$3,500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$4,500–$7,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$6,750–$10,500</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Three patterns stand out. First, the gap between the cheapest and most expensive option on a two-car garage is roughly $6,700 — from $175 for one-part paint to $7,000 for professional polyurea — for floors that can look nearly identical the day the job finishes. Second, professional polyaspartic and professional polyurea sit close together in price, since both use similar spray-applied, fast-cure chemistry and similar contractor labor rates; polyurea usually runs slightly higher because fewer contractors are equipped to apply it. Third, a well-executed DIY epoxy kit on a large garage can cost about the same as the cheapest professional epoxy quote on a one-car garage — size and approach interact in ways that are easy to miss when you only look at one dimension of the comparison.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you've already decided epoxy is your coating and want the full itemized DIY materials list, crack-repair line items, and a detailed savings calculation, see our dedicated <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold no-underline hover:underline">Epoxy Garage Floor Cost</a> guide — this article stays focused on comparing across coating types rather than repeating that breakdown.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 2: Per-Square-Foot Pricing -->
        <div id="per-sq-ft" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Per-Square-Foot Pricing Compared
          </h2>
          <p>
            Total project cost is useful for budgeting, but per-square-foot pricing is the number that actually lets you compare quotes and products apples-to-apples, since it strips out garage size as a variable. Here's how the four coating categories stack up.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
              <thead class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Coating Type</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Cost Per Sq Ft</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Includes Labor?</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Expected Lifespan</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">One-Part Paint</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$0.35–$0.60</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No (DIY)</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1–2 years</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">DIY Epoxy Kit</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$0.50–$1.60</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No (DIY)</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3–20 years*</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Professional Epoxy</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$3–$8</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5–15 years</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Professional Polyaspartic</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$8–$12</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15–25+ years</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Professional Polyurea</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$9–$14</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20–30 years</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">
            *DIY epoxy lifespan varies widely by solids content and whether a topcoat is applied — water-based kits without a topcoat land at the low end, 100% solids kits with a topcoat at the high end. See the <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold no-underline hover:underline">epoxy cost guide</a> for the full breakdown by product tier.
          </p>

          <p>
            A few things worth noting about how these numbers behave in practice. One-part paint's per-square-foot cost looks attractive, but it isn't a coating in the same sense as the other three — it doesn't chemically bond to concrete the way two-part epoxy, polyaspartic, and polyurea do, so it wears through in tire-traffic lanes fast regardless of how carefully it's applied. For a full comparison of paint products specifically, see our <a href="/best-garage-floor-paint/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold no-underline hover:underline">Best Garage Floor Paint</a> guide.
          </p>
          <p>
            Polyaspartic and polyurea per-square-foot pricing overlaps more than most people expect. Both are spray-applied, both cure in a matter of hours rather than days, and both are typically installed by specialty flooring contractors rather than general painters. The practical difference between them comes down to chemistry — polyurea tolerates a wider temperature range during application and stays more flexible over time, which matters most in climates with large seasonal swings. Our dedicated guides on <a href="/polyaspartic-floor-coating/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold no-underline hover:underline">Polyaspartic Floor Coating</a> and <a href="/polyurea-garage-floor-coating/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold no-underline hover:underline">Polyurea Garage Floor Coating</a> go deeper on the performance differences if you're deciding between the two.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 3: DIY vs Pro Cost Gap -->
        <div id="diy-vs-pro-gap" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The DIY vs. Professional Cost Gap
          </h2>
          <p>
            Across every coating type in this guide, professional installation costs somewhere between 4 and 15 times what the equivalent DIY materials cost. That gap isn't padding — it reflects diamond-grinding equipment that runs $3,000–$8,000 to own, commercial-grade coatings priced well above consumer retail, 10–16 man-hours of skilled labor on a typical two-car garage, and a warranty that shifts the risk of a failed application from you to the contractor.
          </p>
          <p>
            The size of the gap also depends heavily on which coating you're comparing. DIY isn't really an option for polyaspartic or polyurea at the professional level — the fast-cure spray chemistry and application equipment those systems use aren't sold to consumers, so "DIY polyaspartic" in practice means a DIY epoxy kit with a polyaspartic topcoat, not a true polyaspartic base system. Epoxy is the one category where DIY and professional are genuinely comparable products, just applied differently, which is why the DIY-vs-pro decision matters most there.
          </p>
          <p>
            If epoxy is the coating you're leaning toward, our <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold no-underline hover:underline">Epoxy Garage Floor Cost</a> guide walks through a full itemized DIY materials list, a real two-car garage savings calculation ($1,865 in a mid-market scenario), and the time investment required — roughly 12–14 hours across two days. Rather than repeat that breakdown here, this guide focuses on how the DIY-vs-pro gap compares across coating types, since that's the piece most cost guides skip.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-6">DIY-to-Professional Cost Multiple — Two-Car Garage (~500 sq ft)</p>
            <div class="space-y-4">
              <div class="flex justify-between items-start border-b border-white/10 pb-4">
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold text-white">One-Part Paint</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">DIY only — professional installation of one-part paint is rarely offered</p>
                </div>
                <span class="text-[#38BDF8] font-black whitespace-nowrap ml-4">~$175–$300</span>
              </div>
              <div class="flex justify-between items-start border-b border-white/10 pb-4">
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold text-white">Epoxy: DIY Kit vs. Professional Basic</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">$250–$800 DIY vs. $1,500–$4,000 professional</p>
                </div>
                <span class="text-[#38BDF8] font-black whitespace-nowrap ml-4">~5–6x</span>
              </div>
              <div class="flex justify-between items-start">
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold text-white">Epoxy: DIY Kit vs. Full Polyaspartic System</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">$250–$800 DIY vs. $4,000–$6,000 professional polyaspartic</p>
                </div>
                <span class="text-[#38BDF8] font-black whitespace-nowrap ml-4">~10–15x</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 4: Cost Drivers -->
        <div id="cost-drivers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Drives Cost Up, Regardless of Coating
          </h2>
          <p>
            Whether you end up with paint, epoxy, polyaspartic, or polyurea, the same handful of factors push the price of any garage floor coating project up. Understanding these matters more than agonizing over which coating to choose, because a contractor's line-item quote is shaped by these variables long before the coating material itself enters the picture.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Surface Prep and Diamond Grinding</h3>
          <p>
            Surface preparation is the single biggest cost driver across every coating type. A clean, sound slab in good condition takes 4–6 hours to diamond-grind and prep. A heavily stained floor with oil penetration, previous coating failures, or surface spalling can take twice as long, and most contractors add a $200–$600 surface condition premium for it. If your concrete has an existing coating that needs to be stripped first, expect an additional $0.50–$1.50 per square foot — on a two-car garage that's $250–$750 regardless of what goes on afterward.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Crack Repair</h3>
          <p>
            Most concrete floors more than 10 years old have some cracking. Hairline cracks can sometimes be bridged by a thick coating application, but anything wider than 1/16 inch needs to be filled first — DIY epoxy crack filler runs $15–$30 per tube, while professional pressure-injected polyurethane caulk for structural cracking adds $200–$400. Budget at least $30–$400 for crack repair depending on severity, and expect this cost whether you're applying paint or polyurea.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Moisture Vapor Barrier Needs</h3>
          <p>
            If your slab tests positive for moisture vapor transmission, any coating applied without addressing it will eventually delaminate from hydrostatic pressure pushing up from below — this is true for epoxy, polyaspartic, and polyurea alike. A moisture-tolerant primer or dedicated vapor barrier coating runs $40–$80 per gallon for DIY versions, and professional moisture remediation adds $300–$800 to a quote. This isn't optional if your floor tests positive; it's a prerequisite step, not an upsell.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Regional Labor Rates</h3>
          <p>
            Where you live swings professional installation costs by 40–60% for identical work. Floor coating labor in California, New York, Massachusetts, and the Pacific Northwest runs well above the national average, so a $2,500 epoxy quote in a mid-cost market can become $3,500–$4,000 for the same job in San Francisco or Boston. This affects epoxy, polyaspartic, and polyurea proportionally — always get three local quotes, since the spread between the lowest and highest bid on identical work often exceeds $1,000.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Garage Size and Minimum Job Charges</h3>
          <p>
            Larger garages generally cost less per square foot because mobilization, setup, and cleanup time gets spread across more floor area. Most professional contractors also carry a minimum project charge — commonly $600–$900 — which means a small one-car garage can end up costing more per square foot than a two-car garage using the identical coating system. If you have an unusually small or oddly shaped garage, ask contractors directly whether a minimum charge applies before comparing quotes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Decision Framework -->
        <div id="decision-framework" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. DIY vs. Pro Decision Framework
          </h2>
          <p>
            Once you've picked a coating category, the DIY-vs-professional decision comes down to a handful of honest questions rather than pure cost comparison.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choose DIY If:</h3>
          <p>
            You're set on paint or epoxy — the two categories where consumer-grade products genuinely exist. Your slab is structurally sound with no active moisture issues and no more than minor cracking. You have at least one weekend free and are comfortable following a multi-step process carefully, including thorough surface prep. You want to save $1,500–$3,000+ compared to a professional epoxy install and are willing to trade roughly 12–14 hours of physical labor for it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choose Professional Installation If:</h3>
          <p>
            You want polyaspartic or polyurea — both require spray equipment and fast-cure formulations that aren't sold to consumers, so professional installation is effectively the only route to a true system in either category. Your floor has active moisture vapor transmission or two or more prior coating failures, both of which point to issues a professional needs to diagnose and address. Your garage is a commercial space, showroom, or high-value area where a coating failure would be costly. You want a long warranty and a single-day (or single-weekend) turnaround with no risk of a DIY misstep.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-6">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-amber-700 mb-2">The Honest Middle Ground</p>
            <p class="text-amber-900">If you're torn, consider hiring a contractor for diamond-grind surface prep only — the step most homeowners genuinely can't replicate — then applying a DIY epoxy system yourself on top of professionally prepared concrete. Some contractors will quote prep-only work. You get the hardest part done right while still saving on materials and finish-coat labor.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Cost Per Year -->
        <div id="cost-per-year" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cost Per Year of Protection: Where the Real Value Is
          </h2>
          <p>
            Upfront cost alone is a misleading way to compare coating types, since a product that costs three times as much but lasts eight times as long is the cheaper option over a real ownership horizon. The table below divides total installed cost by expected service life for a two-car garage across every coating category in this guide.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
              <thead class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Coating System</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Total Cost (500 sq ft)</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Expected Lifespan</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Cost Per Year</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">One-Part Paint (DIY)</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$225</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1–2 years</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">$113–$225/yr</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">DIY Epoxy Kit + Topcoat</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$500</td>
