                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">DIY 100% Solids Epoxy + Topcoat</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$750</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15–20 years</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-green-700">$38–$50/yr</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Professional Epoxy</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2,750</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5–15 years</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">$183–$550/yr</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Professional Polyaspartic</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$5,000</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15–25 years</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">$200–$333/yr</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Professional Polyurea</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$5,750</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20–30 years</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-green-700">$192–$288/yr</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two conclusions follow from this table. First, one-part paint is the worst long-term value in the entire comparison despite being the cheapest upfront — its short lifespan means you're effectively repainting every year or two, which adds up to more than a properly installed epoxy system over a decade. Second, a well-executed DIY epoxy system with a topcoat is the standout value across the entire category, landing at $38–$71 per year versus $183 and up for any professional option. The professional systems — especially polyaspartic and polyurea — earn back some of that gap through much longer service life, but they don't out-earn a good DIY epoxy job on pure cost-per-year math; what they buy instead is certainty of outcome, warranty coverage, and your weekend back.
          </p>
          <p>
            As with the epoxy-specific numbers, this math assumes the coating is properly applied. A DIY project that fails early due to inadequate surface prep costs you twice — once for materials, once to strip and redo — which is why thorough prep is the actual foundation of DIY value, not an optional step to rush through.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What's the cheapest garage floor coating that actually holds up?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">A two-part water-based epoxy kit is the cheapest option that delivers real, lasting protection, running $120-180 for a one-car garage. One-part floor paint is cheaper upfront at $50-90 for a one-car garage, but it isn't a true coating system — it has no chemical bond to concrete, wears through in high-traffic areas within 1-2 years, and needs recoating on a near-annual basis in a daily-driver garage. If you want the lowest true cost of ownership rather than just the lowest sticker price, a DIY epoxy kit beats one-part paint even though it costs more on day one.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How much more does polyaspartic cost than epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Professional polyaspartic systems run $8-12 per square foot installed versus $3-8 per square foot for professional epoxy, so a two-car garage typically costs $1,500-2,000 more for polyaspartic than for a comparable epoxy system. The premium buys faster cure time (often ready for vehicle traffic in 24 hours versus 3-7 days for epoxy), better UV stability so it won't yellow, and a longer expected lifespan of 15-25+ years versus 5-15 years for epoxy. On a cost-per-year basis the gap narrows considerably because polyaspartic lasts so much longer.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is polyurea worth the extra cost over polyaspartic and epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Polyurea is typically the most expensive professional option at $9-14 per square foot installed, slightly above polyaspartic. It's worth the extra cost primarily for garages that see extreme temperature swings, heavy moisture exposure, or high foot and vehicle traffic, since polyurea stays flexible across a wider temperature range and resists moisture-related failures better than epoxy or polyaspartic. For a typical residential garage in a moderate climate, the performance difference between top-tier polyaspartic and polyurea is small enough that price and contractor availability usually decide the choice rather than raw performance.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does garage size change the cost per square foot?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Yes. Per-square-foot pricing generally drops as garage size increases because mobilization, equipment setup, and minimum-job charges get spread across more square footage. A contractor might charge $6 per square foot for a small one-car garage but $4.50-5 per square foot for the same coating system on a three-car garage. Most professional installers also have a minimum project charge — often $600-900 — which makes very small garages proportionally more expensive per square foot than the mid-size jobs they're set up to run efficiently.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What drives garage floor coating costs up the most, regardless of material?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Surface preparation is the single biggest cost driver across every coating type. Diamond grinding a heavily stained or previously coated floor can double prep time and add $200-750 to a quote no matter which coating goes on top. After prep, the next biggest factors are crack repair (an extra $30-400 depending on severity), moisture vapor barrier treatment if the floor tests positive ($300-800 professionally), and regional labor rates, which swing identical jobs by 40-60% between low-cost and high-cost markets. The coating material itself is often a smaller share of total cost than these preparation-related factors.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

