        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Garage Floor Coating Cost Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Coating quotes for the same garage routinely differ by a factor of four, and almost all of that is preparation and thickness rather than profit.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Bids With Different Surface Preparation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Diamond grinding costs more than acid etching and is what makes the coating last. A cheap bid is very often a bid to etch. Ask every contractor how they will profile the slab and compare only bids that specify mechanical grinding.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Asking How Many Mils Go Down</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thin roll-on systems and full-build flake floors are both sold as "coatings" and differ several times over in material. Finished thickness in mils, and the number of coats, is the number that explains the price. Get it in writing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Budgeting Without Slab Repair</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cracks, spalling, pitting, and control joints all need treating before a coating goes on, and they are frequently quoted as extras or discovered once grinding starts. Ask what the contractor expects to find and what those repairs cost before you commit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overlooking the Cost of Downtime</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An epoxy system can keep the garage out of use for the better part of a week while it cures; a polyaspartic install is often back in service the next day. If the garage is the only parking, that difference has a real cost that belongs in the comparison.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Garage floor coating costs</strong> track preparation and thickness far more than brand. A DIY kit runs a few hundred dollars, a professional thin-build coating typically lands in the low thousands for a two-car garage, and a full-thickness flake or polyaspartic system sits above that.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            To compare quotes meaningfully, ask three questions of every bidder: how will you profile the slab, how many mils go down, and what happens if the moisture test comes back wet. A grinding-based bid at a higher price is usually the cheaper floor over ten years.
          </p>
